  Let's say we're in a Cartesian plane,
  with x on the horizontal axis and  ƒ(x)  on the vertical one.
  The range is  ]a , b]  —— a and b are values of x:
  the first and the last values x will take during the integration.
  /*
    Actually  x = a  doesn't happen inside the integral.
    It's the starting point. The point number zero.

    A graduated ruler shows 1 millimetre at the second marker,
    because a distance needs two extremities.
    So n millimetres on the ruler will always be at marker  n + 1 .
  */
  So the integration horizontally spans from a to b.
  So:

    span  =  b — a

  Now what is the differential (dx)?
  It's also a value of x: the constant increment in x,
  a horizontal segment stacked by the integration
  over all its previous selves up to  x = b .
  So  ƒ(x)dx  is the area of a rectangle
  with two horizontal sides dx
  and two vertical sides  ƒ(x) .
  (Which is why integration is used to compute the area under curves ——
  —— special areas that can be negative.)
  ƒ(x)dx  is also stacked like dx, except in 2-D.

  How many slices do we stack?
  It depends on how many times you can fit dx inside span.
  Let's call this number of slices nb.
  Then:
                        b — a
    nb  =  span/dx  =  ———————
                         dx

    <=>    dx  =  span/nb

    <=>    span  =  nb·dx

  /*
    nb must remain an integer,
    so even though it never appears in integral notation,
    it's defining dx, not the other way around.
  */

  Then, what's an average value?
  Usually: an arithmetic mean.
  E.g.: 2, 3, 5 and 7
  —— four values (that's our nb).
  Add all values together and divide by nb:

   sum  =  2 + 3 + 5 + 7

                       2 + 3 + 5 + 7
   avg  =  sum/nb  =  ———————————————
                             4

        =  17/4  =  16/4 + 1/4  =  4.25

  Now what would sum be in our integral?

    sum  =  ƒ(a + dx) + ƒ(a + 2dx) + ... + ƒ(b — dx) + ƒ(b)

  The only difference compared to the integral is that we don't multiply each term by dx.
  Instead of stacking rectangles, we're stacking vertical segments.
  So:

    int  =  sum·dx    <=>    sum  =  int/dx

  Then:
                                     int/dx
    avg  =  sum/nb    =>    avg  =  —————————  =  int/span
                                     span/dx

