  //  Back to Fourier!
  What if we want to retrieve the complex amplitude of a partial?
  Let's start with a simple case: the signal is only one complex sinusoid,
  and its amplitude and phase are constant:

    ƒ(t)  =  X(ω) exp(jωt)

    <=>    X(ω)  =  ƒ(t)/exp(jωt)

                 =  ƒ(t) exp(—jωt)

  Let's expand Euler's formula and see what it means in terms of cosine and sine:

    exp(jωt)exp(—jωt)  =  { cos(ωt) + j·sin(ωt) }{ cos(ωt) — j·sin(ωt) }

                       =  cos²(ωt) — j·cos(ωt)·sin(ωt) + j·cos(ωt)·sin(ωt) — j²·sin²(ωt)

                       =  cos²(ωt) + sin²(ωt)

  (Remember Pythagoras:  opposite² + adjacent²  =  hypotenuse²    <=>    cos² + sin²  =  1 )

     exp(jωt) exp(—jωt)  =  1        //  ok.

  /*
    This, by the way, is a special case of:

      z·conj(z)  =  ( Re(z) + j·Im(z) )( Re(z) — j·Im(z) )

                 =  Re(z)² — j·Re(z)Im(z) + j·Re(z)Im(z) + Im(z)²

                 =  Re(z)² + Im(z)²  =  |z|²
  */

  Now let's assume that our spectrum contains several partials in a harmonic relationship,
  all constant over time in amplitude and phase.
  Harmonic means that their frequencies are related by integer factors:

                                         //  where ω₀ is the fundamental frequency (in rad./s),
             N                           //  N is the number of harmonics,
    ƒ(t)  =  ∑ α[n]cos( nω₀t + φ[n] )    //  N — 1  is the number of overtones,
            n= 1                         //  α[n]  are the real amplitudes,
                                         //  φ[n]  are the phases.

  ω₀ is an angular frequency in radians per second.
  Its value in Hertz is  f₀ = ω₀/2π .
  Its corresponding period is  T₀ = 1/f₀ = 2π/ω₀ .
  Each partial has an angular frequency  n ω₀ ,
  which, in Hertz gives:  f_n  =  nω₀/2π
  and a period:  T_n = 2π/nω₀ .
  If we integrate  ƒ(t)  with respect to t over the range [0 , T₀],
  the fundamental will have reached a full cycle
  and its positive and negative half will cancel out.
  If we integrate over a time window that is an integer multiple of T₀
  (say  μT₀ , where μ is a strictly positive integer),
  the fundamental will have reached μ full cycles and cancel out as well.
  Since T₀ is an integer multiple of T_n, all overtones will also cancel out.
