  So, really, we have one parameter:  ω₀  ——
  —— the fundamental frequency —— or the lowest frequency in the spectrum.
  Or: since each partial's frequency is an integer multiple of ω₀,
  add it to one partial's frequency, you get the next partial's frequency;
  so, in the frequency domain, ω₀ is the distance between any two partials:
  let  p[n]  and  p[n+1]  be two consecutive partials in our spectrum,
  and let  ω[n]  and  ω[n+1]  be their respective frequencies.
  Then:

    #  p[n]  =  cos( ω[n]t + φ[n] )
             =  cos( nω₀t + φ[n] )

    #  p[n+1]  =  cos( ω[n+1]t + φ[n+1] )
               =  cos( (n + 1)ω₀t + φ[n+1] )

    =>    ω[n+1] — ω[n]  =  (n + 1)ω₀ — nω₀
                         =  nω₀ + ω₀ — nω₀
                         =  ω₀

  So it's the difference between consecutive frequencies ——
  —— so, basically a non-infinitesimal differential.
  Its limit values are:

    (1)  ∞  ——>  our spectrum is infinitely ultrasonic
                 and its partials are infinitely apart;

    (2)  0 (DC)  ——>  our spectrum's fundamental is a constant
                      and its partials are infinitely close to each other.

  Case (1) is pretty much the answer to
  "what's the most useless mathematical model of which an engineer can think?"...
  Case (2) is a non-harmonic signal with a continuous spectrum.
  That's interesting.
  Discrete vs. continuous doesn't have to be a dichotomy:
  continuous is a limit-case of discrete.
  Then, instead of non-infinitesimal differential,
  ω₀ is a non-necessarily-infinitesimal differential.
  In syllogistic, when reversing propositions, necessity becomes contingency,
  which, in a deterministic universe,
  is indistinguishable from potentiality.
  So ω₀ is a potentially infinitesimal differential.
  Choose your favourite adverb (your favourite accident),
  it's *essentially* (in the most rigorously literal meaning) ——
  —— a differential.

