  Let's go full-on first principles.

  //  Change
  Let  ƒ(x)  be some function of some variable x;
  and a and b be any values of x (they can be equal if they will).
  Let's agree to stick to the largest set of numbers we need: complex numbers
  (which include real numbers, positive integers, etc.).

  When x evolves from a to b
  (I didn't write "increase" because it could very well be a decrease or a stagnation) ——
  ——  it goes from  x = a  to  x = b .
  Since:    b  =  b + a — a  =  a  +  (b — a) ,
  going from a to b is adding  (b — a)  to a.
  Then  (b — a)  is the change of x between a and b.
  Let's call it dx.
  Then:

    b  =  a + dx

  So:    ƒ(b)  =  ƒ(a + dx)
  Which means that, when x changes from a to b,
  ƒ(x)  changes from  ƒ(a)  to  ƒ(b)  =  ƒ(a + dx) .
  The difference between  ƒ(b)  and  ƒ(a)  is:

    ƒ(b) — ƒ(a)  =  ƒ(a + dx) — ƒ(a)

  That's the change of  ƒ(x)  when x goes from a to b.
  Let's call it dƒ.

  Since a and b can be anything, we could decide that:  a = x .
  Then dx would express the change of x from any value;
  and  ƒ(x + dx) — ƒ(x)  would be the corresponding change of  ƒ(x) .

  //  Rate
  The Latin verb "reor, reri", whose past participle is "ratus, -a, -um", means
  "to reckon, to compute, to think, to judge, ..." —— basically: "to use a brain".
  It gave "ratio": "reason" (sometimes loosely confused with "cause"),
  which passed to English and German with the meaning of "arithmetical division".
  Reor (or rather its participle ratus, -a, -um) also gave "rate"
  which describes a proportional relationship between two things.

  E.g.:  if there are 1,000,000 flat-earthers amongst 8,000,000,000 Earthers:

       1,000,000           1
    ———————————————  =  ———————  =  1/8  E—3  =  0.125·0.001
     8,000,000,000       8,000

    =  0.000125    is the rate of flat-earthers.

  Times 100 gives you a percentage:  0.0125 % .

  Or, if you've walked 1 km in ten minutes:

     1,000
    ———————  =  10/6  =  1 + 2/3 m/s  is your average walking rate.
      600

  (A frequency —— cycles or radians per second —— is therefore a rate.
  Just like any speed.)

  //  Rate of change
  If we want to know how much  ƒ(x)  changes in proportion of a corresponding change in x,
  we'll get:

     dƒ       ƒ(x + dx) — ƒ(x)        //  The ratio of changes,
    ————  =  —————————————————        //  commonly referred to as *rate of change*
     dx             dx                //  or first derivative.

