  //  Calculus
  The big idea of Calculus is to pick two points on a non-linear function,
  assume it's linear between these two points,
  repeat at fixed horizontal intervals to get an approximation;
  then reduce the interval as much as possible to get a more precise approximation;
  the ultimate limit being zero.
  But dividing by dx when dx equals zero won't help,
  so we explicitly express it as a limit.

  (We're sticking to Riemann integrals for now,
  so continuity is a requirement of integration until further notice ——
  —— real numbers as well.)
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_integral
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard_Riemann

