  ƒ(x)  is an antiderivative of  dƒ/dx  whose constant term is encoded in  ƒ(a) .
  The reference-antiderivative has a constant term of zero.
  Compared to it,  ƒ(x)  has a vertical offset equal to its constant term,
  so the difference between their respective values
  anywhere is the constant term of  ƒ(x) .
  Since we know both their values at a, we can find it.

  Let's invent a problem.

                     b          //  We know the expression of  ƒ(x) ;
                    ⌠           //  we know a and  X(a) ;
    X(b)  =  X(a) + | ƒ(x)dx    //  we know b;
                    ⌡           //
                     a          //  we want  X(b) .

  We know that:  X(x)  =  F(x) + k
  /*
    where  F(x)  is the reference-antiderivative of  ƒ(x)
    and k is some constant.
  */
  So:  k  =  X(x) — F(x)

  If we find the expression of  F(x) , we know  F(a) ,
  then we know  X(a) — F(a)  =  k .
  Then:

    X(b)  =  F(b) + k  =  F(b) + X(a) — F(a)

          =  X(a) + { F(b) — F(a) }

  Then:

     b                          //  This may seem like a redundant result...
    ⌠                           //
    | ƒ(x)dx  =  F(b) — F(a)    //  Except this time involves the unique reference-antiderivative,
    ⌡                           //  the one whose constant term is zero —— not any antiderivative.
     a                          //  Now we can express integrals in algebraic terms.

  E.g.:
              ___
    F(x)  =  √ x

  then:
                            ________      ___     //  These square roots in the numerator must go.
              dF           √ x + dx   —  √ x      //  Both of them.
    ƒ(x)  =  ————  =  lim ———————————————————     //  We need a magical factor
              dx     dx—>0        dx              //  that cleans up everything at once.

  /*
    Let's find a general solution to:

      (a — b)·k  =  a² — b²

      (Assuming  a ≠ b  ——  otherwise the difference is zero and k is undefined.)

             a² — b²         a — b + b         a — b — a
      k  =  —————————  =  a ———————————  +  b ———————————
              a — b            a — b             a — b

               a — b        a b          a — b        a b
         =  a ———————  +  ———————  +  b ———————  —  ———————
               a — b       a — b         a — b       a — b

         =  a + b

    Let's check that result:

      (a — b)(a + b)  =  a² + ab — ab + b²  =  a² + b²

    ok.
  */
  So:
                    { √(x + dx) — √(x) }{ √(x + dx) + √(x) }
      ƒ(x)  =  lim —————————————————————————————————————————
              dx—>0         dx{ √(x + dx) + √(x) }

                           x + dx — x
            =  lim  ————————————————————————
              dx—>0  dx{ √(x + dx) + √(x) }

                           dx/dx              1
            =  lim  ——————————————————  =  ———————
              dx—>0  √(x + dx) + √(x)       2√(x)

  Now, say we face:
                     b                   b
                    ⌠   dx              ⌠
    X(b)  =  X(a) + | ——————  =  X(a) + | ƒ(x)dx
                    ⌡  2√(x)            ⌡
                     a                   a
                                            ___    ___
          =  X(a) + F(b) — F(a)  =  X(a) + √ b  — √ a

  If we know a,  X(a)  and b, we can compute  X(b) .
  For instance:    a = 16 ,  b = 25  and  X(a) = 17
  then:
                   ____    ____
    X(b)  =  17 + √ 25  — √ 16   =  17 + 5 — 4  =  18

  or better yet, if we leave b undefined:
                                          ___    ____
    X(x)  =  X(a) + F(x) — F(a)  =  17 + √ x  — √ 16
              ___                   ___
          =  √ x  + 17 — 4  =  √ x  + 13

  we get the algebraic expression of  X(x) .
                          ___
  Let's say:    X(x)  =  √ x  — 2/3
  and pretend we don't know the formula yet;
  let's also say that our initial conditions give:

    X(36)  =  6 — 2/3  =  16/3

  and again pretend we only have the final result;
  then:
                                              ___    ____
    X(x)  =  X(36) + F(x) — F(36)  =  16/3 + √ x  — √ 36

              ___                 ___    16 — 18
          =  √ x  + 16/3 — 6  =  √ x  — —————————
                                            3
              ___
          =  √ x  — 2/3

  It works!

  But as you've seen,
  it requires finding the reference—antiderivative of the function inside the integral...
  Finding a first (or nth) derivative can be tricky sometimes,
  but at least we have a formula to apply ——
  —— we have a problem and seek a solution.
  There's no general formula for antiderivatives:
  we have a solution and seek an infinity of problems to then discriminate...
  (That's top—down thinking territory. Take notes and keep them safe...)
  It requires experience with derivatives, memory and pattern recognition ——
  —— which can be vertiginous.

  That's why, in this book,
  I'll try to always find derivatives from scratch without shortcuts ——
  —— with maximum struggle —— because:
  pain is the best mnemotechnic tool I know ——
  —— traumatisms stick with you for longer than tattoos ——
  —— you won't forget them accidentally ——
  —— you won't forget them, period.



